Yes, every single one of these tools has an official website where you can safely download the correct architectural variant for your system.
When navigating to these sites from your laptop, the web pages will often automatically detect that you are running Linux x86-64 (amd64) and highlight the correct button for you. However, it is always a good practice to double-check before downloading.
Here are the official, verified web destinations for each category:

🌐 Official Websites for Developer Tools & Runtimes

• Java (JDK): oracle.com or adoptium.net (for open-source OpenJDK builds).
• Node.js: nodejs.org
• Go Programming Language: go.dev
• Python: python.org
• Docker: docker.com

🌐 Official Websites for Virtualization & Databases

• VirtualBox: virtualbox.org
• Android Studio: android.com
• MongoDB: mongodb.com
• PostgreSQL: postgresql.org

🌐 Official Websites for Everyday Apps (.deb files)

• Visual Studio Code: visualstudio.com
• Google Chrome: google.com
• Discord: discord.com

⚠️ A Critical Warning for Linux Users

While these official sites exist, you should generally avoid downloading raw files from websites on Ubuntu unless absolutely necessary.
Whenever possible, use your terminal to pull directly from official Linux repositories. This handles your architecture automatically and keeps the apps updated securely. For example:
• Instead of downloading VS Code from the website, run: sudo snap install code --classic
• Instead of downloading Docker manually, use Docker's official guide to add their automated apt repository.
